  "title": "The Lost Reservation",
  "category": "food-drink",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 6,
  "order": 605,
  "theme": "food",
  "culturalNote": "Booking a table (kuweka meza / reservation) is common for dinners out in Nairobi. When something goes wrong, Kenyan service staff will smooth it over with \"Pole kwa usumbufu\" (sorry for the trouble) and a bit of patience on both sides. The -me- and -ja- tenses carry this whole scene: \"nimefika\" (I've arrived), \"sijaona jina lako\" (I haven't seen your name yet), \"imeisha\" (it's finished — sold out).",
  "startingPoint": "You arrive at a restaurant in Westlands for dinner. You booked a table yesterday — or did you?",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "perfect-me",
    "perfect-negative-ja",
    "comparatives"
  ],
